---
import { site } from '../site.config';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';

const title = `About Muncie Radon | How We Work & Who Does the Work`;
const description = `Muncie Radon connects Delaware County property owners with IDOH-certified radon professionals. How the free estimate works and why we publish our pricing.`;
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <PageHero
    h1={`About ${site.brand}`}
    intro={`${site.brand} is a lead referral service for radon testing and mitigation in ${site.city} and ${site.region}. We connect property owners with ${site.localFacts.certifier}-certified radon professionals, and we publish what things cost so you can decide whether to call at all.`}
    crumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}
    placement="hero:about"
    formHref="/contact/"
  />

  <div class="wrap-narrow prose about-body">
    <h2>What we are, stated plainly</h2>
    <p>
      Most local service websites are vague about this, so here it is up front. <strong
        >{site.brand} is a lead generation brand.</strong
      > When you call the number on this site or send the quote form, you reach us, and we route your
      job to an independent radon professional who holds {site.localFacts.certifier} certification and
      works in {site.county}.
    </p>
    <p>
      {site.state} requires certification from the {site.localFacts.certifierFull} to provide radon
      testing and mitigation services to the public. That certification belongs to the professional
      doing the work on your property, not to this website, and we verify it before we route anyone
      to you.
    </p>

    <Callout tone="info" title="Why we say this out loud">
      <p class="mb-0">
        Plenty of sites in this category are structured exactly like this one and describe themselves
        as though they own trucks. We would rather tell you and be trusted on the parts that follow.
        If working through a referral service is not what you want, the alternative is straightforward:
        search the {site.localFacts.certifierFull} certified professional list directly and call
        someone from it. That is a completely reasonable thing to do.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2>How the free estimate works</h2>
    <p>
      A radon quote does not require a sales appointment to produce a range. It requires a sales
      appointment to produce a <em>final number</em>, which is a different thing, and conflating the
      two is how this industry wastes people's evenings.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>You call or send the form.</strong> Two required fields. If you have a test result
        already, that alone tells us most of what matters.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>We ask about the house.</strong> Foundation type, footprint, whether there is an
        existing system, and whether you are working against a deadline.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>You get a range for your situation</strong>, not the generic one on this page.
        Usually on that first call.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>If it makes sense to proceed</strong>, an on-site assessment produces the fixed
        quote. Ask for it in writing, from us or from anyone else you call.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The estimate is free whether or not you hire anyone, including in the cases where the honest
      answer is that your number does not justify the spend right now.
    </p>

    <h2>Why we publish pricing</h2>
    <p>
      Search radon mitigation for this city and you will find directories, a national brand's
      auto-generated city page, and a handful of companies based an hour or more away. Almost none of
      them will tell you a number without a form.
    </p>
    <p>
      We publish <strong>{site.priceRange}</strong> for a typical single-family system because the
      alternative wastes everyone's time. If that range does not fit your budget, you found that out
      in ten seconds instead of after an appointment. If it does, you start the conversation already
      knowing roughly where it lands, which is a better conversation.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same principle drives the rest of the site. Every question we get asked is answered on the
      page rather than held back as a reason to call: what a system costs, whether a 3.3 pCi/L
      reading is worth fixing, what happens when a radon test fails mid-transaction, and how to tell
      if the system you already have has quietly stopped working.
    </p>

    <h2>What we will not tell you</h2>
    <p>
      A short list, because the omissions on a site like this are as informative as the claims:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>We do not claim years in business, a team, or a review count.</strong> This is a new
        brand. Inventing history would be the easiest lie on the internet and the first one worth
        catching.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>We do not publish testimonials we did not receive.</strong> When there are real ones,
        they will go up with real names attached.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>We do not use the scarier radon research to sell systems.</strong> The lung cancer
        link is well established and is the reason to act. Claims tying radon to other conditions are
        far less settled, and leaning on them would be using your fear against you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>We do not pretend every reading needs mitigation.</strong> Below 2.0 pCi/L, retest in
        two years and spend your money elsewhere.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why {site.city}</h2>
    <p>
      {site.county} is EPA Zone 1, the highest radon risk designation the agency assigns, and the
      average {site.city} home tests at about {site.localFacts.avgLevel} pCi/L, which is exactly the
      level at which the EPA says to take action. That combination means roughly half the housing
      stock in this county is sitting above the action level.
    </p>
    <p>
      What the county does not have is a dedicated local radon resource. The search results are
      directories and out-of-town companies. This site exists to be the {site.city}-specific
      answer: local pricing, local geology, local transaction practice, and a phone number that
      reaches a person.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions we are happy to answer on the phone</h2>
    <p>
      Including the ones that do not lead to a sale. Whether your existing system is working, whether
      your inspection report actually says what the agent said it says, whether a borderline reading
      is worth acting on, and what a reasonable quote from anyone looks like. Call {
        site.phone.display
      } or use the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <CtaBand placement="footer-cta:about" formHref="/contact/" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-body {
    padding-block: var(--s-7);
  }
  .about-body h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
